module std.tui

// Box drawing, as three strings a caller assembles.
//
// Pieces rather than a `draw_box(x, y, w, h)`, for the reason the whole library is shaped
// around: a frame is built as text and printed once, so anything that positions the cursor
// and writes on its own cannot be part of one. These return rows, and rows are what
// `Renderer` takes.
//
// **One character set, single-line.** A caller who wants double lines or heavy rules writes
// three functions of their own — they are one concatenation each, and the alternative is
// this file growing a style enum to save nobody any work. What is worth having in a library
// is the arithmetic, which is where the off-by-ones live.
//
// Every piece is exactly `width` columns, so a box composes into a frame whose rows must be
// full width (see `Renderer`). The corner glyphs are one column each, as are `─` and `│`.

/// The top edge: `┌───────┐` at exactly `width` columns.
///
/// # Panics
/// Traps if `width` is less than 2, which cannot hold the two corners. Unlike `fit`, there
/// is no smaller box to return — the borders *are* the box, so a width that cannot hold
/// them is a wrong call rather than a tight one, and a layout that computed it will not be
/// improved by silently drawing something else. Nearly every real width here is a constant
/// or comes from `terminal_size()`, which answers 80x24 rather than something degenerate
/// when there is no window.
pub let box_top = pure (width: i64) -> string => "┌" ++ (rule(interior(width)) ++ "┐")

/// The bottom edge: `└───────┘`. Traps on a width under 2, as `box_top` does.
pub let box_bottom = pure (width: i64) -> string => "└" ++ (rule(interior(width)) ++ "┘")

/// A content row: `│ text  │`, the text fitted to the space between the borders.
///
/// The text is `fit` to `width - 2`, so it is cut when long and padded when short and the
/// right border always lands in the same column. An escape sequence inside `text` breaks
/// that, exactly as it breaks `fit` — it occupies no columns while counting as runes — so
/// colour goes *around* the whole row rather than inside it.
///
/// # Panics
/// Traps on a width under 2, as `box_top` does.
pub let box_row = pure (text: string, width: i64) -> string =>
  "│" ++ (fit(text, interior(width)) ++ "│")

/// A top edge carrying a title: `┌─ Title ────┐`.
///
/// The title is cut to whatever room is left after the corners, the two rule characters
/// that frame it and its two spaces, so a long title shortens the rule to nothing rather
/// than pushing the corner off the row. That is the `fit` rule applied to a smaller
/// interior, and it is why the width arithmetic is worth having in one place.
///
/// # Panics
/// Traps on a width under 2, as `box_top` does.
pub let box_top_titled = pure (title: string, width: i64) -> string => {
  // Called for the trap; the interior is not the arithmetic this form needs.
  let _ = interior(width)
  // Five columns are spoken for whatever the title is: the two corners, the `─` and space
  // that introduce it, and the space that closes it. What is left is the room the title
  // and the trailing rule share.
  let room = width - 5
  if room <= 0 {
    box_top(width)
  } else {
    // `fit` to the *smaller* of the two, so a short title is not padded out to the whole
    // room — that would push the rule off the end and leave the corner one column late.
    let shown = fit(title, title.len().min(room))
    "┌─ " ++ (shown ++ (" " ++ (rule(room - shown.len()) ++ "┐")))
  }
}

/// `n` horizontal rule characters, or the empty string when `n` is not positive — a box
/// exactly two columns wide has no interior, which is a real box and not an error.
let rule = pure (n: i64) -> string => {
  var out = ""
  for _ in 0..<n { out = out ++ "─" }
  out
}

/// The interior width — what is left after the two borders — having checked there is room
/// for them.
///
/// Every public form goes through this, which is what makes the trap a property of the
/// file rather than of the three functions that remembered to check. `box_top(1)` returned
/// a two-column `┌┐` while its own documentation said it trapped, for exactly as long as
/// the check lived at one call site.
///
/// # Panics
/// Traps if `width` is less than 2. The message is a constant, so nothing here allocates
/// beyond the string it is building.
let interior = pure (width: i64) -> i64 => {
  if width < 2 { panic("std.tui: a box needs at least 2 columns for its borders") }
  width - 2
}
